.lua
book.chapter(35, "DSLtk.hpp: composition and feature mixins")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Understand the toolkit beneath the language"
.pp
DSLtk.hpp is a header-only C++20 toolkit for constructing domain-specific
languages. Most names live in namespace dsl; the generic REPL also has the
dsltk namespace alias used by surrounding documentation. MetaML adds its
own grammar, type system, evaluator, and module semantics on top of reusable
facilities. When reading the implementation, distinguish what a toolkit can
provide from which facilities a particular MetaML path actually uses.
.pp
The header contains several complementary abstractions rather than one
mandatory object hierarchy. Feature mixins can be composed through dsl::DSL,
while parsers, graphs, terms, streams, and other facilities can also be used
directly. A language author can choose only the relevant components. Adding
a generic graph algorithm belongs in the toolkit; assigning meaning to a
MetaML constructor belongs in the language layer.
.sh 2 "CRTP supplies a static composition point"
.pp
The curiously recurring template pattern passes the final derived type to
a base template. DSL takes that derived type and a list of feature templates.
Each feature contributes operations to the composed type, avoiding a central
runtime registry of every possible DSL capability. This is host-language
metaprogramming: C++ templates construct an interface at compilation time,
while the resulting operations can still process runtime values.
.lua
book.code("cpp", [=[#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <cassert>
struct Arithmetic : dsl::DSL<Arithmetic, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};
int main() {
    auto value = Arithmetic::wrap(5)
        | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x + 3; })
        | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x * 2; });
    auto positive = Arithmetic::make_pred([](int x) { return x > 0; });
    assert(value == 16);
    assert(positive(value));
}]=], "A complete feature-composed C++ client")
.endlua
.pp
The D counterpart uses template mixins and native template syntax rather
than C++ inheritance spelling. DSLtk.d provides the corresponding feature
families at module scope. The important shared idea is composing a small
domain interface from reusable operations. Native syntax and reflection
facilities differ, so source-level similarity is not the criterion for
parity; the resulting operation's behavior is.
.sh 2 "Pipeline adapters expose data flow"
.pp
Pipe wraps a callable as a stage. Chaining a value through stages expresses
ordinary function composition at the C++ level. This resembles MetaML's |>
notation conceptually but is a separate host API with C++ overload resolution,
types, and lifetime rules. Do not assume a host pipeline inherits MetaML's
numeric wrapping, type inference, or diagnostic conversion merely because
the visual flow looks familiar.
.pp
Use explicit parameter and return types when a stage boundary is difficult
to understand. Generic lambdas can make adapters reusable, but also increase
the distance between a mistaken type and a compiler diagnostic. A thin
domain wrapper can expose meaningful operations while keeping template
machinery inside its implementation. That is the purpose of the toolkit:
users of the resulting DSL should not need to understand every feature mixin.
.sh 2 "Predicates and static names"
.pp
The Operators family composes predicate-like operations. A predicate wrapper
gives ordinary callables a vocabulary for conjunction, disjunction, and
negation. Keep side effects out of predicate composition unless the exact
evaluation order is part of the contract. A validation rule is easier to
reason about when its only observable result is its boolean answer.
.pp
Fixed strings support compile-time names used in tagged AST construction,
pattern dispatch, and other template APIs. A tag such as num is known to
the compiler even though a leaf's payload can be runtime data. This
separation enables specialized dispatch without requiring the entire parsed
program to exist at compile time. Static names are a vocabulary mechanism,
not a claim that all language processing is constant evaluation.
.lua
book.code("cpp-fragment", [=[auto tree = dsl::node<"add">(
    dsl::leaf<"num">(2), dsl::leaf<"num">(3));]=], "Compile-time tags with runtime payloads in C++")
book.code("d-fragment", [=[auto tree = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(2), leaf!"num"(3));]=],
    "The corresponding D tag syntax")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Expression templates and other feature families"
.pp
Expression templates build host expression objects whose evaluation can be
deferred or fused according to their implementation. They are distinct from
MetaML's runtime expression AST and from a generic tagged ASTNode. Choose
the representation according to purpose: a numeric host DSL may benefit
from expression templates, while an editor or interpreter needs inspectable
syntax with source information. Similar words such as expression and node
can describe different layers with different ownership contracts.
.pp
The header also contains optional-value and Result helpers, memoization,
lazy values, task pipelines, enum reflection, PEG facilities, serialization,
preprocessing, and graph-oriented language tools. Read the relevant public
contract before combining them. Memoizing an effectful operation changes
behavior, a lazy value delays work but may retain captures, and a runtime
tree owns different data from a parser cursor borrowing source bytes.
.sh 2 "Build small native experiments"
.pp
A focused translation unit is often the fastest way to understand a toolkit
facility. Include only the public header, construct a minimal example, and
assert a semantic result. Compile with the project's actual C++20 requirement.
When a feature exists in both dialects, write a corresponding D example or
consult the mirrored native tests. Keep those experiments separate from
claims about MetaML syntax: host template code is not executable MetaML.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Start with DSLtk.hpp's feature list, examples/01-basic-usage.cpp, and the
toolkit manual under DSLtk/manual/DSLtk. Compare DSLtk.d's mixin-based
composition with the C++ CRTP interface. Build a two-stage host pipeline
that validates and formats a small domain value. Explain which work occurs
at template instantiation and which occurs when the pipeline runs, then
identify the lifetime of every object captured by its callbacks.
